.TH COMM 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
comm \- select or reject lines common to two sorted files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comm
.RB [ \-123iz ]
.RB [ \-\-output\-delimiter= \fIstr\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-total ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-order | \-\-nocheck\-order ]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B comm
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
which should be sorted in byte order, and writes three columns to
standard output:
lines only in
.IR file1 ,
lines only in
.IR file2 ,
and lines in both.
Each column is preceded by one delimiter (a tab by default) for every
earlier column that is not suppressed.
.PP
Either operand may be
.B \-
for standard input.
Lines are compared byte by byte with
.BR strcmp (3)
(or case-insensitively with
.BR \-i );
the locale's collation order is not used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-1
Suppress column 1 (lines only in
.IR file1 ).
.TP
.B \-2
Suppress column 2 (lines only in
.IR file2 ).
.TP
.B \-3
Suppress column 3 (lines in both files).
.TP
.B \-i
Compare lines case-insensitively.
A common line is printed as it appears in
.IR file1 .
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
Lines are terminated by NUL bytes instead of newlines, on input and
output.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-delimiter= str
Separate columns with
.I str
instead of a tab.
.TP
.B \-\-total
After the output, print a summary line containing the number of
lines in each of the three columns, separated by the column
delimiter, followed by the word
.BR total .
The counts include suppressed columns.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-order
Report the first out-of-order line found (in either file) with a
diagnostic on standard error; later disorder is not reported.
Processing continues, but the exit status is 1.
.TP
.B \-\-nocheck\-order
Do not check input order (the default).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-check\-order
was given and an input was not sorted.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, a file could not be opened, or a write error occurred.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print lines that appear in both files:
.PP
.EX
$ comm \-12 a.sorted b.sorted
.EE
.PP
Print lines only in the first file:
.PP
.EX
$ comm \-23 a.sorted b.sorted
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR join (1),
.BR strcmp (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024 for
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-2 ,
and
.BR \-3 .
.B \-i
follows BSD; the long options and
.B \-z
follow GNU coreutils.
.SH BUGS
Giving
.B \-
for both operands is not rejected; the second read of standard input
sees end of file.
